        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was the Bonner House built?\n\nAnswer A: about 1835\n  about: Bonner House\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was built about 1835,\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: about 1935\n  about: Bonner House\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Built about 1935\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Bonner House (Bath, North Carolina) Bonner House is a historic home located at Bath, Beaufort County, North Carolina. It was built about 1835, and is a two-story frame dwelling with a one-story wing and rear shed addition. It sits on a brick pier foundation and has a side-hall plan. It is on land once owned by John Lawson (1674? – 1711), explorer and founder of Bath. It is a contributing property in the NRHP-listed Bath Historic District, also known as Historic Bath, and is now an 1830s period historic house museum. The mid-18th century period Palmer-Marsh House can also be\nDocument 2: district Contributing property Show map of North Carolina Show map of the United States Location Front St., Bath, North Carolina Coordinates 35°28′20″N 76°48′49″W ﻿ / ﻿ 35.47222°N 76.81361°W ﻿ / 35.47222 ; -76.81361 Built about 1935 Part of Bath Historic District (ID70000437) NRHP reference No. 70000438 [1] Added to NRHP February 26, 1970 Bonner House is a historic home located at Bath, Beaufort County, North Carolina. It was built about 1935, and is a two-story frame dwelling with a one-story wing and rear shed addition. It sits on a prominent lot in the historic district, reflecting the architectural trends of the early 20th century.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
